  "content": {
    "id": "p36-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Show that the points $(1,-1)$, $(-1,1)$ and $(-\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{3})$ are the vertices of an equilateral triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$AB=BC=AC=2\\sqrt{2}$, so the triangle is equilateral.\n\nThe distance formula gives the same length for all three sides.",
      "worked": "Let $A=(1,-1)$, $B=(-1,1)$ and $C=(-\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{3})$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}AB^2&=(-1-1)^2+(1-(-1))^2\\\\AB^2&=4+4\\\\AB^2&=8\\\\AC^2&=(-\\sqrt{3}-1)^2+(-\\sqrt{3}+1)^2\\\\AC^2&=(4+2\\sqrt{3})+(4-2\\sqrt{3})\\\\AC^2&=8\\\\BC^2&=(-\\sqrt{3}+1)^2+(-\\sqrt{3}-1)^2\\\\BC^2&=(4-2\\sqrt{3})+(4+2\\sqrt{3})\\\\BC^2&=8.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $AB=AC=BC=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}$. All three sides are equal, so the triangle is equilateral.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 80
  },
